{"name":"Golden Tiger","hex":"#e1a451","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"golden","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of, or relating to, gold.","Having a color or other richness suggestive of gold."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["(Can we verify this sense?) brassy drummer, brassy chub, etc. (Kyphosus vaigiensis), a fish found in southeast Asia.","Ellipsis of golden retriever."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/golden"},{"term":"tiger","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Panthera tigris, a large predatory mammal of the cat family, indigenous to Asia.","A male tiger; as opposed to a tigress."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A final shouted phrase, accompanied by a jump or outstretched arms, at the end of a cheer."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tiger"}],"entries":[{"title":"Golden tiger","blocks":[{"text":"A golden tiger, sometimes called a golden tabby tiger, is a Bengal tiger with a colour variation caused by a recessive gene. Like white tigers and black tigers, it is a morph, and not a separate subspecies. Known for its blonde or pale-golden color and red-brown stripes, the golden tiger colouring comes from a recessive trait referred to as \"wideband\" which affects the production of black during the hair growth cycle. Tiger colorations that vary from the typical orange-with-black-stripe do occur in nature, but in a very small percentage."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Golden_Tabby_weibchen_Cheryl.jpg/330px-Golden_Tabby_weibchen_Cheryl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_tiger","wikidata":"Q740122"},{"title":"Tiger","blocks":[{"text":"The tiger is a large cat and a member of the genus Panthera native to Asia. It has a powerful, muscular body with a large head and paws, a long tail and orange fur that fades to white in parts, with black, mostly vertical stripes. It is traditionally classified into nine subspecies, though some recognise only two subspecies, the mainland Asian tiger and the Sunda Islands tiger."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Bengal_tiger_%28Panthera_tigris_tigris%29_female_3_crop.jpg/330px-Bengal_tiger_%28Panthera_tigris_tigris%29_female_3_crop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiger","wikidata":"Q19939"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Golden_tiger_3_-_Buffalo_Zoo.jpg/500px-Golden_tiger_3_-_Buffalo_Zoo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Golden_tiger_3_-_Buffalo_Zoo.jpg/1280px-Golden_tiger_3_-_Buffalo_Zoo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Golden_tiger_3_-_Buffalo_Zoo.jpg/500px-Golden_tiger_3_-_Buffalo_Zoo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Golden tiger in Buffalo Zoo","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGolden_tiger_3_-_Buffalo_Zoo.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Tiger_distribution.png/500px-Tiger_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Tiger_distribution.png/1280px-Tiger_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"h